b/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a06cdb67-1683-534c-8615-e83f9147b2fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467882} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6b7678-25c3-5224-a960-9698f38fdb8c" :answer-field/key "${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}<6}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8018b9d7-b35d-54ca-b97a-1ac935d3c057" :question/math-academy-id "q-71815" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,4).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open *since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }1\\le x<3,\\\\\\,2<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048439 